  "narrative": {
    "intro": "The Department of Agriculture obligated $8B in contracts in FY2026 to date. The agency awarded 23,287 contracts with an average award size of $343K. Obligations fell 16.6% year over year. Annual obligations rose from $9.7B in FY21 to $11.6B in FY24, then dropped to $9.6B in FY25 and to $8B in FY26. The largest spending categories in FY2026 to date are Nonscheduled Chartered Freight Air Transportation at $877M, Support Activities for Forestry at $500M, and Fruit and Vegetable Canning at $477M. Other major category commitments include Highway, Street, and Bridge Construction at $473M, Animal (except Poultry) Slaughtering at $447M, and Poultry Processing at $402M. The top vendors by obligations in FY2026 to date include KIEWIT INFRASTRUCTURE SOUTH CO at $261M, PERIMETER SOLUTIONS LP at $227M, and TYSON FOODS INC. at $176M. The mix of categories and vendors shows a combination of transportation, construction, food processing, forestry support, and IT services driving the agency’s contract spending in the fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How has Department of Agriculture contract spending trended over recent years?",
        "body": "The Department of Agriculture obligated $9.7B in FY21, $10B in FY22, $11.5B in FY23, $11.6B in FY24, $9.6B in FY25, and $8B in FY26 to date. Obligations fell 16.6% year over year into FY2026 to date. The FY2026 total is a decline from the FY24 peak of $11.6B. The award count in FY2026 to date is 23,287 with an average award size of $343K."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries and vendors dominate Agriculture contract obligations in FY2026 to date?",
        "body": "Nonscheduled Chartered Freight Air Transportation leads industry categories at $877M, followed by Support Activities for Forestry at $500M and Fruit and Vegetable Canning at $477M. Other top categories include Highway, Street, and Bridge Construction at $473M, Animal (except Poultry) Slaughtering at $447M, and Poultry Processing at $402M. KIEWIT INFRASTRUCTURE SOUTH CO is the largest vendor at $261M, followed by PERIMETER SOLUTIONS LP at $227M and TYSON FOODS INC. at $176M."
      }
    ]
  },
  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How much does Department of Agriculture spend on contracts?",
      "a": "The Department of Agriculture obligated $8B in contracts in FY2026 to date. The agency awarded 23,287 contracts with an average award size of $343K. Obligations fell 16.6% year over year from FY25 to FY26 to date. Annual obligations by fiscal year were $9.7B in FY21, $10B in FY22, $11.5B in FY23, $11.6B in FY24, and $9.6B in FY25."
    },
    {
      "q": "What does Agriculture buy?",
      "a": "The Department of Agriculture buys a mix of transportation, forestry, food processing, construction, and IT services. Top categories in FY2026 to date are Nonscheduled Chartered Freight Air Transportation at $877M; Support Activities for Forestry at $500M; Fruit and Vegetable Canning at $477M; Highway, Street, and Bridge Construction at $473M; Animal (except Poultry) Slaughtering at $447M; Poultry Processing at $402M; Computer Systems Design Services at $369M; Other Computer Related Services at $358M; Cheese Manufacturing at $354M; and All Other Miscellaneous Chemical Product and Preparation Manufacturing at $229M."
